   308	/**
   309	 * What a READER actually receives, given a Telegram parse mode.
   310	 *
   311	 * Review pass 33 finding 1, proven by execution before repair: the guard ran on the PRE-FORMAT source
   312	 * while the formatter then changed the representation. A payload whose only content characters sat in a
   313	 * link DESTINATION passed — `[<zero-width>](https://example.com/x)` — and went on the wire as
   314	 * `<a href="https://example.com/x">\u200b</a>` with `parse_mode: HTML`. Tags stripped, the reader
   315	 * received one zero-width space. The original incident's exact harm, through the one door nobody had
   316	 * looked at, inside the guard built to close that harm.
   317	 *
   318	 * The reasoning that failed is worth naming: "the source contains a visible code point, therefore the
   319	 * reader receives content" does not survive a representation change. Whatever transforms the message
   320	 * last is what decides what a reader sees.
   321	 *
   322	 * This extraction is DELIBERATELY SMALLER than a renderer, and review pass 35 is why. An earlier version
   323	 * grew toward modelling Telegram's two parsers — decoding character references, consuming emphasis
   324	 * delimiters — and every approximation error became an OVER-refusal: `~` is not a delimiter in legacy
   325	 * Markdown, and `zwnj`/`nbsp`/`shy`/`apos` are not entities Telegram's HTML mode resolves, so payloads
   326	 * whose visible content was literal punctuation were being judged empty. An over-refusal destroys a real
   327	 * message. Rather than chase a parser with regexes, this keeps only the two transforms that are true of
   328	 * Telegram's rendering when the markup is VALID — and that qualification is load-bearing, because
   329	 * review pass 36 finding 5 showed the previous sentence ("without qualification") was false:
   330	 *
   331	 *   HTML     — a tag is markup; its attributes are never shown.
   332	 *   Markdown — a link displays its LABEL; the destination is not shown.
   333	 *
   334	 * KNOWN OVER-REFUSAL, named rather than discovered (pass 36 finding 5). The HTML branch strips every
   335	 * TAG-SHAPED substring without establishing that Telegram would accept it as a tag. If a payload
   336	 * carries malformed or unsupported tag-shaped text — which a reader WOULD see, because Telegram
   337	 * rejects the parse and falls back to sending the source — plus one invisible text node, the
   338	 * extraction is non-empty and invisible, so this refuses a message that would have been readable.
   339	 * The refusal is terminal, so that message is lost rather than downgraded.
   340	 *
   341	 * It is not fixed here for the same reason the under-refusals below are not: deciding it requires
   342	 * Telegram's own parse result, and every regex approximation of that parser has produced errors in
   343	 * BOTH directions (passes 34, 35, 36). It rides CMT-1260 with them.
   344	 *
   345	 * KNOWN UNDER-REFUSALS, accepted deliberately. A character reference (`&#8203;`) is counted as its
   346	 * SOURCE characters, so a payload made only of encoded invisibles passes. Emphasis delimiters count as
   347	 * content, so `*\u200b*` passes. Each is a payload that reaches a reader as nothing — the very harm this
   348	 * file exists to prevent — and each is allowed anyway, because refusing them requires a rendering model
