docs/eli16/durable-outbound-dedup.eli16.md:5:## The problem (the duplicate messages Justin spotted)
docs/eli16/durable-outbound-dedup.eli16.md:7:During the "server temporarily down" instability, a red-team test message hit Echo right as the server was restarting. The same reply went out **5 times, byte-for-byte identical**, within 19 seconds. Echo has a duplicate-suppressor, but it kept its "recently sent" list purely in memory — and a restart (or two server processes briefly overlapping during the churn) means that in-memory list is empty/split, so the repeats slipped through.
docs/eli16/durable-outbound-dedup.eli16.md:11:A small on-disk store for the duplicate-suppressor's fingerprints. Now when Echo is about to send a reply, it checks both its in-memory list AND the on-disk record — so an identical reply sent moments ago is caught even if the server restarted in between.
docs/eli16/durable-outbound-dedup.eli16.md:15:A duplicate-suppressor that *wrongly* blocks a real reply is worse than the duplicate it prevents. So the on-disk store can never block a send: if the database is missing, locked, corrupt, or its native library is broken, every operation quietly does nothing and Echo falls back to exactly the old in-memory behavior. It can only ever *add* a catch, never drop a legitimate message. (The native-database fragility was itself part of this incident, so even opening the store is guarded.)
docs/eli16/durable-outbound-dedup.eli16.md:20:- It catches the duplicate *send*; a deeper guard to stop the message from being *re-processed* across a restart in the first place is noted as a follow-up.
docs/eli16/durable-outbound-dedup.eli16.md:32:`tests/unit/outbound-dedup-durable.test.ts` (6 tests): catches a duplicate across a simulated restart (fresh instance, same db file — the actual bug); in-memory still works; different text/topic/past-window are NOT suppressed; fail-open on a throwing store and on an unwritable path. The existing dedup tests (11) still pass; `tsc` clean. causalAutopsy: the dedup state was in-memory-only, which the restart churn (this incident) exposed.
docs/eli16/apprenticeship-ux-gate.eli16.md:6:confusing notices, the duplicate messages, the "please resend that" asks.
docs/eli16/apprenticeship-ux-gate.eli16.md:24:cycle now REQUIRES an "operator-seat UX" block: how many duplicate notices
docs/signal-vs-authority.md:17:Low-context filters can't distinguish legitimate inputs from the specific failure mode they were built to catch. "Test" is a debug token *sometimes* and a legitimate word *most of the time*. A similarity score of 0.8 is a near-duplicate *when the respawned session re-generates the same answer* and a natural echo *when the user asked to hear the point again*. No brittle check can tell these cases apart. If we give brittle checks blocking authority anyway, we produce over-blocks, then fix them with more brittle checks, then over-block more, forever.
docs/signal-vs-authority.md:67:- **`OutboundDedupGate`** *(resolved)* — was wired as a direct 422-block authority in `server/routes.ts`. Rework: it is now a pure signal (`signals.duplicate`) passed into `checkOutboundMessage()`. The tone gate decides based on the full picture.
docs/eli16/pending-inject-durability.eli16.md:19:Delivery is at-least-once: if the server dies after typing the message but before removing the record, the next boot delivers a duplicate. A duplicated message to an agent is harmless noise; a silently dropped user message cost an hour last night. We chose the duplicate.
