docs/eli16/post-drive-transcript-audit.eli16.md:7:The apprenticeship loop already had rules telling agents to watch the user's experience, but rules written only in prose were easy to miss. The UX-blindspot arc proved that duplicate delivery notices, resend asks, restart chatter, and empty status updates could happen in front of the operator without becoming durable findings. This change turns that duty into a repeatable audit over the transcript itself.
docs/eli16/post-drive-transcript-audit.eli16.md:17:This adds a small command for post-drive audits. Given one or more topic ids and a time window, it reads the topic messages, filters to that window, classifies four UX antipatterns, prints a structured report, and writes each finding to the framework issue ledger. The four classes are duplicate notices or deliveries, asks of the user to resend or retry, internal infrastructure noise, and content-free progress updates.
docs/enforcement-gaps.json:224:          "stale_note": "count corrected 86 → 82 on 2026-08-09: review pass 8 found this field stale while the lint reported clean, because the sweep digest covers the ARTICLE and not the cited data. A count duplicated out of its source will drift from it."
docs/enforcement-gaps.json:618:        "how": "Found by an INDEPENDENT reviewer (the laptop lane) by execution, not by reading, and reproduced end-to-end here before any repair. A seventh standard was appended to the registry under a heading IDENTICAL to one of the six already fingerprinted, carrying its own fingerprint line. `scripts/lint-enforcement-fingerprint.mjs` printed clean at seven fingerprinted, with no objection to the repeated name. `scripts/lint-enforcement-gap-records.mjs` objected only SIDEWAYS — that a content digest had changed — and its remedy line said to re-sweep and paste the new digest. **Complying with the guard's own printed instruction was the step that completed the exploit:** both guards then reported clean, five gaps swept, against a live population of seven, while the five sweeps between them reached a verdict on six. All four existing arms miss it for a reason worth recording, because 'we have four checks' was exactly the feeling of coverage: the staleness arm asks whether a live NAME is absent from the swept set and both entries share the name; the partition arm compares the sweep against its own recorded population, which is a set of names, so it partitions perfectly; the content-address arm is the one that should catch it and is precisely the one the collision defeats, since a Map holds one digest per name; and the floor arm has no opinion about articles. The second direction was then demonstrated too: with the collision in place, the ORIGINAL article's fingerprint was rewritten to read `moments: none; surfaces: none whatsoever` and NOTHING went stale. **Honest bound on the evasion:** the build never actually went green — `scripts/lint-no-duplicate-definitions.mjs` refuses a repeated article heading and fails first, which was verified against the attacked tree rather than assumed. But that coverage was BORROWED: it parses the registry separately and, unlike the two fingerprint guards, unwraps blockquotes before matching, so the three populations agreed by coincidence and nothing recorded or re-checked the coupling.",
docs/enforcement-gaps.json:657:            "why": "This is where the shape was found, and the repair for it took THREE attempts, each one an instance of the shape it was fixing. (1) A duplicate-name check covering only the half of the collision space where the duplicate ALSO carries a fingerprint — review pass 10 walked the other half through. (2) That check plus a partition assertion certified as the real general repair — review pass 11 proved the partition UNREACHABLE on every input (9,330 modelled populations, zero reaching it), because the name check preempted it. (3) CONSOLIDATION: the partition alone refuses both halves, so the name check contributed no coverage and only preempted the partition into silence. Two checks were never two things. What stands in the frozen tree is ONE refusal, whose condition is the partition arithmetic and whose message names the duplicate headings. It is NOT a broader net than name-uniqueness, and not an equal one: it catches STRICTLY LESS. A duplicate heading where NEITHER article carries a fingerprint leaves the identity satisfied, so the partition never fires; that case is caught by the shrink-only baseline arm and the sibling duplicate-definitions lint. Corrected at review pass 26 finding 4, which proved it by input and noted that the evidence field of this very verdict had stated it all along while this field said the opposite.",
docs/enforcement-gaps.json:658:            "evidence": "Re-reached 2026-08-09 after review pass 12 found this field still describing a \"name arm\" that the freeze commit had DELETED, and citing as proof a source-mutation my own commit body had already disowned. The current evidence, all fired by INPUTS rather than by editing the guard: attack A (duplicate heading, both fingerprinted) refused — \"89 article(s), but 6 fingerprinted + 82 without a fingerprint = 88\"; attack B (duplicate heading, the new article carrying no fingerprint) refused identically; a heading indented one to three spaces refused by the dialect check, with fenced and HTML-commented examples correctly NOT refused; the untouched registry clean at 88 / 6 / 82. NEGATIVE CONTROL BY REMOVAL, which is the first honest one this arm has had: delete the surviving refusal and BOTH attacks report clean. KNOWN NOT COVERED, stated rather than left to be found: a duplicate heading where NEITHER article carries a fingerprint is not caught by this refusal — it is caught by the shrink-only baseline arm and by the sibling duplicate-definitions lint, so net coverage is unchanged, but the credit belongs to the chain and not to this guard.",
docs/enforcement-gaps.json:671:            "why": "Immune deliberately rather than by luck, which is the distinction this sweep exists to draw. Its cited surface `scripts/write-audit-convergence.mjs` builds a registry article index and REFUSES the collision up front: a repeated article title returns `duplicate standards article title ... is ambiguous`, and a repeated Article ID returns a duplicate-owner error, both before the index is built. The audit slug itself cannot collapse either — the slug must equal the file basename, so the filesystem holds one record per slug and a second audit under one slug is a visible overwrite rather than a silent merge. **RE-REACHED 2026-08-11.** The digest moved because a tree-node acknowledgement sentence was appended to this article naming its new child (*A Metric Must Measure the Work, Not the Question*). That edit changed narrative structure — parentage — and touched neither the moments, the surfaces, nor the coverage argument a sweep evaluates, so the verdict above is re-reached unchanged rather than re-stamped. The digest is deliberately over-sensitive; that is the check forcing a look, and the look was taken.",
docs/enforcement-gaps.json:696:            "why": "VERIFIED rather than asserted, because this shape's whole point is a duplicate that both admits an unexamined entry and silently unwatches an earlier one. The article ID `metric-measures-the-work-not-the-question` and the countdown id `STD-COUNTDOWN-metric-measures-the-question` each appear exactly once as declarations, and the countdown lint's own parser — the authority, not a raw text count — reports 4 article countdowns and 48 distinct sub-obligation countdowns with no collision. A raw grep does show the parent's `STD-SUBCOUNTDOWN-audit-never-started` twice; the second is the CORRECTION NOTE describing pass 11's historical duplicate, not a declaration, and the lint's population is unaffected.",
docs/enforcement-gaps.json:760:            "why": "Two of its cited surfaces concede on the pass path. The commit gate ends every Tier-1 commit with `No converged spec required for Tier 1` — a routine statement that the converged-spec arm did not run — and additionally prints `note: trace has no duplicateBuildCheck field (duplicate-build guard not live for this build — advisory only)`, which announces an inactive guard in so many words. Both appeared on every commit of this window, roughly fifteen times tonight alone, and I read past both. That is the shape precisely: honest text, correct text, emitted beside a success, unattended. **RE-REACHED 2026-08-11.** The digest moved because a tree-node acknowledgement sentence was appended to this article naming its new child (*A Metric Must Measure the Work, Not the Question*). That edit changed narrative structure — parentage — and touched neither the moments, the surfaces, nor the coverage argument a sweep evaluates, so the verdict above is re-reached unchanged rather than re-stamped. The digest is deliberately over-sensitive; that is the check forcing a look, and the look was taken.",
